    "result_text": "The full suite is green (21 tests, 0 failures/errors, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced canonical address normalization for owner creation.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java`: a single shared, idempotent normalizer that trims, collapses whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands abbreviations token-by-token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`.\n\n**Wired into the create pipeline:**\n\n- `ValidateOwnerFields` \u2014 replaced the plain `requireText(\"address\", \u2026)` with a `normalizeAddress` step that normalizes the address, **rejects it as a 400 when blank after normalization**, and writes the normalized value back onto the request. Because this is the single `@RequestBody` step that republishes the DTO, every downstream step (BuildOwner \u2192 SaveOwner \u2192 response) persists and returns the normalized address.\n- `CheckHouseholdUnique` \u2014 the address side of the household duplicate comparison now uses `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)` for both the request and each existing owner (last-name comparison unchanged).\n- `AssignHouseholdId` \u2014 the shared `householdId` hash key now derives the address portion from `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)`, so owners in one household deterministically share an id.\n\nI kept the last-name normalization (lower-case/collapse) as-is since the change was scoped to addresses, and updated the Javadoc on each touched class to match. This maps directly onto the four spec-driven acceptance tests (Cp01 blank-after-normalize rejection, Cp10 duplicate detection, Cp11 shared household id, Cp12 whitespace/case/abbreviation), all of which pass.",
